#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158368} :activity/id #uuid "b37433c3-4f4c-53f8-92b4-d30ca0f2836d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050758 :topic/id #uuid "64548ccd-8386-51d3-93a4-ad6b2dfb666c" :topic/math-academy-id 2915} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158368 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158439 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we’ll explore different ways to measure how far apart two points are in space.\n\nTo formally define a distance between two points $x$ and $y$, we use a **metric function** or **distance function**. This is a rule $d(x, y)$ that assigns a non-negative number to each pair of points, representing how far apart they are.\n\nA valid distance function must satisfy these four properties:\n\n- *Identity*:\n$d(x, x) = 0$\n- *Positivity*:\n$d(x, y) > 0 \\quad \\text{if} \\quad x \\ne y$\n- *Symmetry*:\n$d(x, y) = d(y, x)$\n- *Triangle inequality*:\n$d(x, z) \\leq d(x, y) + d(y, z)$\n\nThese conditions ensure the function behaves like a meaningful measure of distance.\n\nLet’s consider some widely used concrete examples of metrics." :tutorial/id #uuid "0f37d7d5-bb53-5b03-a189-fb4bdba4b82e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10854 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5b859f14-d1c7-53f9-af96-f1617b8bbf3c" :step/math-academy-id 462528 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158422}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158458 :tutorial/content "Although Manhattan and Euclidean distances are defined in different ways, they have something important in common. What matters for both is the difference between the two points, not their absolute positions.\n\nEach one measures the length of the vector connecting the points, just with a different notion of distance. This makes it possible to express both using the idea of a norm.\n\nA **norm** is a function that measures the size or length of a vector.\n\nThere are many types of norms, but two of the most commonly used, and closely related to the distance metrics we just defined, are the **Euclidean norm** and the **Manhattan norm**.\n\n- The **Euclidean norm** (also called the $L^2$ norm) is given by\n$\\left.\\| \\mathbf{v} \\\\right|_2 = \\sqrt{\\sum_{i=1}^N v_i^2}$.\nThis is the usual length of a vector in geometric terms.\n- The **Manhattan norm** (also called the $L^1$ norm) is given by\n$\\\\begin{vmatrix}\\left.\\mathbf{v} \\\\right|_1 = \\sum_{i=1}^N & v_i\\end{vmatrix}$.\nThis gives the total amount of movement in all coordinate directions.\n\nNow we can rewrite our earlier distance formulas using norms. The Euclidean distance becomes\n\n$$\nd_2(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) = \\| \\mathbf{x} - \\left.\\mathbf{y} \\\\right|_2\n$$\n\nand the Manhattan distance becomes\n\n$$\nd_1(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) = \\| \\mathbf{x} - \\left.\\mathbf{y} \\\\right|_1\n$$\n\nThis makes the connection between distances and vector norms clear; distances are just norms of differences.\n\nNow, let's work through a few examples to understand how to compute distances step by step." :tutorial/id #uuid "a8f852dc-20a2-52a4-a2ff-e6a924a28b27" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13163 :tutorial/title "Connection Between Metrics and Norms"} :step/id #uuid "888f1b27-7e2a-5ec7-b041-a5a1af63fb8c" :step/math-academy-id 462529 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158370}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158370 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157433 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157043 :question/id #uuid "25111cb4-1ff7-5514-8dd8-fab7ac30c46f" :question/math-academy-id "e-16638"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04bdd95b-c658-5cfb-a720-246d2eaebbd7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048849}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157708}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Euclidean Distance"} :step/id #uuid "d9070166-5d69-5e87-bf7a-027a75911649" :step/math-academy-id 462530 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158371}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158371 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157434 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157040 :question/id #uuid "c19d702d-7023-5133-9911-9584a4bb3e88" :question/math-academy-id "e-16632"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38d1bdc1-006f-5efa-a4b0-380bd2d331c4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157709}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Manhattan Distance"} :step/id #uuid "acd7fcd3-f6a5-521c-b4e9-eee9c0597af9" :step/math-academy-id 462533 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158372}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158372 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158440 :tutorial/content "We’ve seen how both Euclidean and Manhattan distances can be written in terms of vector norms. These are actually special cases of a more general formula known as the **Minkowski distance**.\n\nGiven a fixed value\n\n$$\np \\ge 1\n$$\n\n, the Minkowski distance between two points $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ in $N$-dimensional space is defined as\n\n$$\nd_p(\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}) = \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^N \\mid a_i - \\left.b_i\\right|^p \\right)^{\\frac{1}{p}}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding **Minkowski norm** is given by\n\n$$\n\\left.\\| \\mathbf{v} \\\\right|_p = \\left.\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^N \\mid v_i\\right|^p \\right)^{\\frac{1}{p}}\n$$\n\nThe parameter $p$ determines how differences in individual coordinates contribute to the total distance:\n\n- **$p=1$** (Manhattan): All differences are summed equally.\n- **$p=2$** (Euclidean): Larger differences have more weight due to squaring.\n- **$p \\to \\infty$**: The distance is determined entirely by the single largest coordinate difference.\n\nLet’s compute an example to see how this works in practice.\n\nSuppose\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = [1,\\ 2,\\ 3]^T\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = [4,\\ 0,\\ 6]^T\n$$\n\n. Let's compute their Minkowski distance using $p = 5$.\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd_{5}(a, b) &= ‖a - b‖_{5} \\\\\n&= ‖\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}‖_{5} \\\\\n&= (\\begin{vmatrix}1 - 4 & ^{5} + & 2 - 0 & ^{5} +\\end{vmatrix}3 - \\left.6\\right|^{5})^{1/5} \\\\\n&= (\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & ^{5} + 2^{5} +\\end{vmatrix}- \\left.3\\right|^{5})^{1/5} \\\\\n&= (243 + 32 + 243)^{1/5} \\\\\n&= 518^{1/5} \\\\\n&\\approx 3.490\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's try a few examples to build intuition." :tutorial/id #uuid "648c4531-3873-59b1-b7ba-962d787aec10" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10855 :tutorial/title "The Minkowski Distance"} :step/id #uuid "556aba2d-b193-5869-9be5-80e44c1a3a2c" :step/math-academy-id 462536 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158373}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158373 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157435 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157041 :question/id #uuid "0c580a08-5a51-516f-9ac8-ca3b0ed9984e" :question/math-academy-id "e-16633"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a3f27e0-ef39-5965-9fe1-d141c944ac66" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157710}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Minkowski Distance"} :step/id #uuid "d86008c6-95b6-5d11-8079-faa1d9e8f580" :step/math-academy-id 462537 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158374}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158374 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158441 :tutorial/content "Now that we’ve learned how the Minkowski norm depends on the parameter $p$, let’s see what happens when we keep increasing $p$.\n\nRecall that the Minkowski norm of a vector $\\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^N$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\left.\\| \\mathbf{v} \\\\right|_p = \\left.\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^N \\mid v_i\\right|^p \\right)^{1/p}\n$$\n\nLet’s explore how this behaves using a concrete example. Consider the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = [1,\\ 2,\\ 3]^T\n$$\n\n.\n\nNow compute its Minkowski norms for increasing values of $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖v‖_{2} &= (1^{2} + 2^{2} + 3^{2})^{1/2} = (14)^{1/2} \\approx 3.742 \\\\\n‖v‖_{5} &= (1^{5} + 2^{5} + 3^{5})^{1/5} = 276^{1/5} \\approx 3.077 \\\\\n‖v‖_{10} &= (1^{10} + 2^{10} + 3^{10})^{1/10} = 60074^{1/10} \\approx 3.005 \\\\\n‖v‖_{100} &= (1^{100} + 2^{100} + 3^{100})^{1/100} \\approx 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $p$ increases, the smaller components contribute less and less to the overall value. The norm becomes increasingly influenced by the largest coordinate, in this case, $3$.\n\nLet’s now understand why this happens more formally. Suppose $\\mathbf{v}$ is a nonzero vector, and that one of its entries is strictly larger than all the others. Let $j$ be the index where $\\begin{vmatrix}v_j & > & v_i\\end{vmatrix}$ for every\n\n$$\ni \\ne j\n$$\n\n.\n\nOur goal is to understand the behavior of the norm as $p \\to \\infty{:}$\n\n$$\n\\left.\\lim_{p \\to \\infty} \\|\\mathbf{v}\\\\right|_p = \\left.\\lim_{p \\to \\infty} \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^N \\mid v_i\\right|^p \\right)^{1/p}\n$$\n\nTo simplify this expression, we factor out\n\n$$\n\\left. \\mid v_j\\right|^p\n$$\n\nfrom the sum inside\n\n$$\n\\lim_{p \\to \\infty} \\\\begin{vmatrix}\\left.\\mathbf{v}\\\\right|_p = \\lim_{p \\to \\infty} & v_j & \\left(1 + \\sum_{i \\ne j} \\left(\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}v_i &}{\\end{vmatrix}v_j\\end{vmatrix}} \\right)^p \\right)^{1/p}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mid v_j \\mid$ is the largest component, each ratio\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\begin{vmatrix}v_i &}{\\mid v_j\\end{vmatrix}}\n$$\n\nis a number less than $1$. When we raise numbers less than $1$ to larger and larger powers, they shrink toward zero.\n\nThat leaves us with\n\n$$\n\\lim_{p \\to \\infty} \\\\begin{vmatrix}\\left.\\mathbf{v}\\\\right|_p = & v_j & \\cdot 1 = & v_j\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis motivates the definition of the **Chebyshev norm** (also called the **max norm** or $L^\\infty$ norm) as\n\n$$\n\\left.\\|\\mathbf{v}\\\\right|_\\infty = \\max\\left(\\begin{vmatrix}v_1 &,\\ & v_2 &,\\ \\ldots,\\ & v_N\\end{vmatrix} \\right)\n$$\n\nIt simply picks out the largest absolute value among the components of $\\mathbf{v}$.\n\nUsing this norm, we define the **Chebyshev distance** between two points $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ as\n\n$$\nd_\\infty(\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}) = \\max\\left(\\begin{vmatrix}a_1 - b_1 &,\\ & a_2 - b_2 &,\\ \\ldots,\\ & a_N - b_N\\end{vmatrix} \\right)\n$$\n\nThis distance reflects the largest difference across coordinates. It’s especially useful in situations where we care about the worst-case difference in any direction.\n\nLet’s work through a few examples to see how this plays out in practice." :tutorial/id #uuid "d2f3719b-5987-5504-b341-6127c1fa5947" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10856 :tutorial/title "The Chebyshev Distance"} :step/id #uuid "d8b7bf73-6c13-58ae-a291-0a81ceab560a" :step/math-academy-id 462540 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158375}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158375 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157436 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157042 :question/id #uuid "b54dd07c-5e48-5d06-a454-c7dd907804d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-16634"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c9c3102-21e9-590d-ad14-c09baba8b4cf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157711}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Chebyshev Distance"} :step/id #uuid "0f82fb25-8038-5302-acac-fd61bcc1b700" :step/math-academy-id 462542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158422 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158464 :tutorial/content "The most familiar example of a metric is the **Euclidean distance**, which represents the straight-line distance between two points.\n\nFor two points $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$, it is defined as\n\n$$\nd_2(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) = \\sqrt{\\sum_{i=1}^N (x_i - y_i)^2}\n$$\n\nEuclidean distance measures the shortest path directly connecting the points, as shown in the figure below.\n\n![](images/a9/a9d42944e5bca8beb26cbc9ed76ef5de5ee72d60319c5077cc472170167eb9e0.png)\n\nNext, we will look at an alternative metric based on grid-like movement." :tutorial/id #uuid "f46aba5e-854c-5efb-aa29-4def4408438e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14939 :tutorial/title "Euclidean Metric"} :step/id #uuid "d6d4a180-9707-5ab0-ae65-ed02b4497cf2" :step/math-academy-id 610560 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158423}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158423 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158465 :tutorial/content "Another common metric is the **Manhattan distance**, also known as **taxicab distance**.\n\nIt is defined as\n\n$$\nd_1(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) = \\sum_{i=1}^N \\mid x_i - y_i \\mid\n$$\n\nManhattan distance measures the total movement along a grid, adding the distance traveled in each coordinate direction rather than cutting across the diagonal. This is shown in the figure below.\n\n![](images/97/97c57d903af41281bc84acb103cb4f8def01dd8a1de5671a32107f58b93f177d.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "29829e18-f334-5b55-8620-a5ee79bd6f5e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14940 :tutorial/title "Manhattan Metric"} :step/id #uuid "99d28ccc-8e1d-531b-8825-3a1c6c7f91cf" :step/math-academy-id 610585 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158369}}] :activity/title "Euclidean, Manhattan, and Minkowski Distance" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157518}]}
